  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Thursday) \nIssued at 930 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nExtended range model solutions suggest that a 500-mb ridge will \nremain positioned across the forecast area on Monday. However, as we \nprogress through the rest of the period, it is becoming increasingly \nlikely the the southern edge of the mid-latitude westerlies will \ndevelop southward into our region, with indications of a flat \nsubtropical ridge lying across the northern Gulf and development of \nan upper low (from phasing of 2-3 distinct northern stream \nshortwaves) over the Great Lakes. In the low-levels, a weak area of \nlow pressure is predicted to migrate from the Mid-South into the TN \nValley on Monday, before degenerating into a frontal wave as it lifts\nnortheastward into the Upper OH Valley Monday night. Although the \ntrailing cold front will make only gradual southeastward progression \ninto the Ozarks and OH Valley by early Tuesday morning, mesoscale \nboundaries originating from extensive convection to our northwest \nshould contribute to a higher coverage of thunderstorms across our \nCWFA Monday afternoon and evening. As surface pressures begin to rise\nwithin a high migrating eastward across southern Ontario/Quebec (and\nrenewed cyclogenesis takes place over the eastern Great Lakes), the \ncold front will advance further southeastward on Tuesday, resulting \nin another episode of convection during the afternoon/evening hours. \nSufficient CAPE will exist to support a risk for strong-locally \nsevere thunderstorms both periods, especially given an increase in \nmid-level flow to 15-25 knots. As the cold front shifts further \nsoutheastward through the region Wednesday, light-moderate northerly \nflow will begin to advect a slightly cooler/drier airmass into the \nregion, with highs on Wednesday and Thursday dropping back into the \n85-90F range and lows falling into the l-m 60s.",
